{"source":"github","project_id":"multilevelmod","target":"tidymodels/multilevelmod","id":"pr/97","ref":97,"title":"Thread data through gee_formula() to support dot in formula","description":"Closes #64.\n\n`gee_formula()` called `terms()` without `data`, so a `.` in the formula failed with `'.' in formula and no 'data' argument`. It now gets `data` from `gee_fit()`. Since `.` would also pull in the column named in `id_var()`, the terms are rebuilt with that column left out, and a `.` formula now gives the same coefficients as the formula written out in full.\n\nThe GEE engine still needs an `id_var()` term, so the reprex in the issue (`vs ~ .` with no id) still errors, now with the message asking for `id_var()`; `vs ~ . + id_var(cyl)` works.\n","published_at":"2026-09-29T02:56:08Z","url":"https://github.com/tidymodels/multilevelmod/pull/97","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"taekop","state":"OPEN"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"multilevelmod","target":"tidymodels/multilevelmod","id":"pr/96","ref":96,"title":"Fix id_var() name in GEE error message","description":"Closes #91.\n\nThe GEE special is `id_var()` everywhere (the `terms()` call, the docs and the tests), but the error for a missing or repeated id column told people to use `id_vars()`, which doesn't exist. The message and its example now say `id_var()`, with a snapshot test for both cases.\n","published_at":"2026-09-29T02:56:03Z","url":"https://github.com/tidymodels/multilevelmod/pull/96","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"taekop","state":"OPEN"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"multilevelmod","target":"tidymodels/multilevelmod","id":"pr/95","ref":95,"title":"Document recipes that rename/replace columns (#75)","description":"This PR adds a section to the Get Started vignette explaining that the formula supplied to add_model() is evaluated on the baked recipe output rather than the original data. Steps such as step_dummy() and step_pca() can rename or replace columns, causing formulas that still reference the original column names to fail.\r\n\r\nThe new section demonstrates this behavior with step_dummy(), contrasts the fragile approach of naming the generated column explicitly with the more robust use of . in the formula, and shows how . adapts to the columns produced by the recipe—including when tuning num_comp.\r\n\r\nCloses #75.","published_at":"2026-09-17T17:02:11Z","url":"https://github.com/tidymodels/multilevelmod/pull/95","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"guadapascal","state":"OPEN"}}
